    {
      "node_id": 0,
      "node_type": "about",
      "title": "The other story, not the untold one",
      "uuid": "b5655daf4c8a4d8b864bb15b4b184cb4",
      "x": 793.0562650445802,
      "y": 1165.0354120186958,
      "width": 366.515625,
      "height": 29.0,
      "z_value": -9.94600000000003,
      "ports_visible": false,
      "shelf_visible": false,
      "label": "The other story, not the untold one",
      "depth_front": false,
      "node_tint": ""
    },
    {
      "node_id": 0,
      "node_type": "warm",
      "title": "Soft",
      "uuid": "27c2385955774950888a22b16389403a",
      "x": 1064.932019188094,
      "y": 346.1039593131719,
      "width": 300.0,
      "height": 553.0,
      "z_value": -9.901000000000055,
      "ports_visible": false,
      "shelf_visible": true,
      "body_text": "Every other model makes you babysit it.\nFable 5 is the first one built to be left alone.\nThat is the entire difference, and it is the reason this article exists. Opus 4.8 is fast and sharp, but it is built for the response you read in a minute. Fable 5 is built for the task that takes hours, the one that plans, spawns its own sub agents, checks its own work, and reports back when it is actually done.\nAnthropic said it plainly in their own guide. Fable 5 is particularly effective at end to end work that takes a person hours, days, or weeks. That is not marketing. That is a spec sheet for a night shift worker.\nSo while everyone else is asking Fable 5 the same quick questions they asked Opus, here are 60 workflows that use it for what it was actually made to do. Long. Autonomous. Unsupervised. Running while you sleep.\nEvery one is a trigger, an agent, and a verification step. Skip the verification and you wake up to expensive mistakes. Keep it and you wake up to finished work. That rule never changes, and with Fable 5 running longer and more autonomously than any model before it, it matters more, not less.\nA note before the list. Fable 5 has one behavioral quirk you must handle on any long run. It can report a step as done before verifying it. Anthropic's own fix, which nearly eliminates this, goes in every autonomous prompt below:\nBefore reporting progress, audit each claim against a tool result from this session."
    },
    {
      "node_id": 0,
      "node_type": "warm",
      "title": "Clear Vision",
      "uuid": "1a40bcb8c8e64c24a1fe6c11e8659978",
      "x": 1664.7695585141196,
      "y": -924.287062818278,
      "width": 300.0,
      "height": 371.0,
      "z_value": -9.898000000000057,
      "ports_visible": false,
      "shelf_visible": true,
      "body_text": "Content Machines\n1. Overnight ingest and publish. Drop a transcript, article, or PDF into a folder. Fable 5 extracts the core ideas, drafts a thread, a post, and a newsletter blurb, and files all three. You wake up to drafts, not a blank page.\n2. Competitor monitor. A daily run that checks five competitor accounts, summarizes anything new, and flags topics you have not covered yet.\n3. Repurposing engine. One long piece in. Ten short variations out. Thread, three posts, a carousel script, a video script.\n4. SEO cluster builder. One seed keyword. Fable 5 maps the full cluster and drafts ten interlinked articles targeting different long tail variations overnight.\n5. Newsletter autopilot. Every morning it drafts your newsletter from what happened in your niche while you slept. You edit for ten minutes and send."
    },
    {
      "node_id": 0,
      "node_type": "warm",
      "title": "Unfold",
      "uuid": "f6453bcb2e824d9b86ae3bc97c2b82af",
      "x": 1189.3108290284433,
      "y": -818.7994137972212,
      "width": 300.0,
      "height": 146.0,
      "z_value": -9.899000000000056,
      "ports_visible": false,
      "shelf_visible": true,
      "body_text": "If a step failed, state that with the output. Do not report success you cannot prove.\nPaste that into the system prompt of anything you leave running overnight. Now the workflows."
    },
    {
      "node_id": 0,
      "node_type": "about",
      "title": "Note",
      "uuid": "08c01d661d6641eba4e17806b28e79cb",
      "x": 1809.5799906888715,
      "y": -351.8872491879265,
      "width": 850.0,
      "height": 29.0,
      "z_value": -9.900000000000055,
      "ports_visible": false,
      "shelf_visible": false,
      "label": "Only report work you can point to evidence for. If something is not verified, say so.",
      "depth_front": false,
      "node_tint": ""
    },
    {
      "node_id": 0,
      "node_type": "about",
      "title": "Note",
      "uuid": "8c6372fef0134b72b872234f44134c4f",
      "x": 2668.4273122207733,
      "y": -865.3079426227821,
      "width": 734.0,
      "height": 29.0,
      "z_value": -9.894000000000059,
      "ports_visible": false,
      "shelf_visible": false,
      "label": "Open one pull request with everything that passed. Before reporting done,",
      "depth_front": false,
      "node_tint": ""
    },
    {
      "node_id": 0,
      "node_type": "warm",
      "title": "Kinetic",
      "uuid": "0af4a615f77e44bea77c49b2c44df622",
      "x": 1673.2023294778785,
      "y": -1193.5118044301564,
      "width": 300.0,
      "height": 206.0,
      "z_value": -9.896000000000058,
      "ports_visible": false,
      "shelf_visible": true,
      "body_text": "Code That Ships Itself\n11. Overnight bug sweep. Point Fable 5 at your repo before bed. It finds and fixes lint errors, failing tests, and obvious bugs, then opens a pull request. This is where Fable 5's long autonomous runs pay off most. Use this prompt:\nWork through this repo. Fix lint errors, failing tests, and clear bugs one at a time."
    },
    {
      "node_id": 0,
      "node_type": "about",
      "title": "Note",
      "uuid": "1264ae1432a64c9c92c43f17804d92ad",
      "x": 2261.273822694152,
      "y": -1161.1832304737443,
      "width": 666.0,
      "height": 29.0,
      "z_value": -9.895000000000058,
      "ports_visible": false,
      "shelf_visible": false,
      "label": "Run the test suite after each change. Only keep changes that pass.",
      "depth_front": false,
      "node_tint": ""
    },
    {
      "node_id": 0,
      "node_type": "warm",
      "title": "Clay",
      "uuid": "376ee4c7c9604b56ab88b12f176a0153",
      "x": 2106.181115820073,
      "y": -841.6294283180505,
      "width": 300.0,
      "height": 326.0,
      "z_value": -9.897000000000057,
      "ports_visible": false,
      "shelf_visible": true,
      "body_text": "Trading and Market Analysis\n6. Wallet pattern scanner. Point it at on chain data. It logs high win rate wallets and their entry and exit timing to a file you review each morning.\n7. Overnight chart review. Queue tickers before bed. Fable 5 pulls price data, runs multi timeframe analysis, and writes a morning briefing.\n8. News to thesis pipeline. Watches headlines, filters for your watchlist, drafts a thesis note on impact before the market fully prices it.\n9. Earnings call summarizer. Drop in a transcript. It extracts guidance changes, tone shifts from the last call, and contradictions with prior statements.\n10. Rebalancing alert. Compares current allocation to target and drafts the exact trades. It never executes. You control every dollar."
    },
    {
      "node_id": 0,
      "node_type": "warm",
      "title": "Boundless Joy",
      "uuid": "5c5303c243bf49cd983a84382693a3a7",
      "x": 2659.438830927765,
      "y": 72.1766761270036,
      "width": 300.0,
      "height": 326.0,
      "z_value": -9.89200000000006,
      "ports_visible": false,
      "shelf_visible": true,
      "body_text": "Sales and Lead Generation\n16. Cold outreach personalizer. Feed it a prospect list. It researches each one and drafts a unique opening line, not a generic mail merge.\n17. Inbound lead qualifier. Reads each form submission, checks it against your ideal customer profile, tags it hot, warm, or cold before you look.\n18. Proposal drafter. After a call, feed it the transcript. It drafts a proposal on your template with the exact pain points and numbers mentioned.\n19. Follow up sequence writer. Give it your stalled deals. It drafts a tailored follow up for each based on how long it stalled.\n20. Prospecting list builder. A scheduled search for businesses matching your criteria, compiled into a daily list."
    },
    {
      "node_id": 0,
      "node_type": "warm",
      "title": "Sweet",
      "uuid": "524c40bf213e412a8f9efd1e775db8c1",
      "x": 2652.4289175367085,
      "y": -264.1271228864923,
      "width": 300.0,
      "height": 296.0,
      "z_value": -10.0,
      "ports_visible": false,
      "shelf_visible": true,
      "body_text": "audit each fix against the actual test output. If tests still fail, say so with the log.\n12. Dependency update loop. Weekly. Updates packages one at a time, runs tests after each, opens a PR only for updates that pass clean.\n13. Documentation generator. After every merged PR, it reads the diff and updates the relevant docs so they never drift.\n14. Landing page variant builder. Your current page plus a list of angles in. Five complete variants out, ready for your testing tool.\n15. Client micro site builder. Find a business with an outdated site. Feed Fable 5 their content. It rebuilds a modern version as a finished proof of concept."
    },
    {
      "node_id": 0,
      "node_type": "warm",
      "title": "Tiny little extra sprinkles of joy",
      "uuid": "2aa58ad212a54a129b0a7234e58461f1",
      "x": 3010.6458777548796,
      "y": 13.01239297209841,
      "width": 363.0,
      "height": 221.0,
      "z_value": -10.0,
      "ports_visible": false,
      "shelf_visible": true,
      "body_text": "Research and Knowledge Work\n21. Second brain ingestion. Drop any source in and say ingest this. Fable 5 reads it, links it to existing notes, and files it into a living wiki that compounds.\n22. Deep research agent. This is Fable 5's strongest solo workflow. It can run sub agents in parallel across a hard question. Use this:\nResearch this question thoroughly. Delegate independent sub questions to sub agents"
    },
    {
      "node_id": 0,
      "node_type": "warm",
      "title": "Becoming Yours",
      "uuid": "1ef7682ad3364c92a45dd52c0daf8243",
      "x": 3884.5752503228787,
      "y": 443.2915810396825,
      "width": 300.0,
      "height": 236.0,
      "z_value": -9.888000000000062,
      "ports_visible": false,
      "shelf_visible": true,
      "body_text": "Do not infer what it would have found.\n23. Meeting notes to action items. Extracts action items from transcripts, assigns them to the people mentioned, drops them into your task manager.\n24. Feedback synthesizer. A month of tickets in. The top five complaints and top five praise points, ranked by frequency, out.\n25. Due diligence first pass. Reads provided documents and flags red flags, inconsistencies, and missing information before you dig in."
    },
    {
      "node_id": 0,
      "node_type": "about",
      "title": "Note",
      "uuid": "eb2908b7adea4d4987bd259ac3ea4f2a",
      "x": 3743.3241561295445,
      "y": 320.4431111250283,
      "width": 850.0,
      "height": 29.0,
      "z_value": -9.889000000000062,
      "ports_visible": false,
      "shelf_visible": false,
      "label": "Synthesize only after all report back. If a sub agent fails, say so in the synthesis.",
      "depth_front": false,
      "node_tint": ""
    },
    {
      "node_id": 0,
      "node_type": "warm",
      "title": "Intentional",
      "uuid": "64b7142b5e9c4256b68b2c862c40c506",
      "x": 3330.2225715837576,
      "y": 269.93153811836004,
      "width": 300.0,
      "height": 116.0,
      "z_value": -9.890000000000061,
      "ports_visible": false,
      "shelf_visible": true,
      "body_text": "and keep working while they run. Each sub agent gets a bounded scope and clear success criteria."
    },
    {
      "node_id": 0,
      "node_type": "warm",
      "title": "Golden Hour",
      "uuid": "6f85e6d058da4b8b824928e3ffc27fb0",
      "x": 4513.4617083393405,
      "y": 1049.943062903184,
      "width": 300.0,
      "height": 311.0,
      "z_value": -9.884000000000064,
      "ports_visible": false,
      "shelf_visible": true,
      "body_text": "Infrastructure That Runs Itself\n41. CI sweeper. Failed builds get investigated. Low risk fixes open a PR. Ambiguous ones escalate with full context.\n42. Changelog drafter. Every tagged release gets a draft changelog from commit history, ready for a light edit.\n43. Post merge cleanup. After a merge, a pass removes dead code, unused imports, and stale flags.\n44. PR babysitter. Watches open pull requests, runs tests, answers simple comments, pings a human when judgment is needed.\n45. Vulnerability sweep. Scans your dependency tree, drafts the patch PR for anything with a fix and a passing test suite."
    },
    {
      "node_id": 0,
      "node_type": "warm",
      "title": "Golden Hour",
      "uuid": "c30798f73dbe4d388c107a96c80b168c",
      "x": 4721.461408439234,
      "y": 1423.2044693742541,
      "width": 300.0,
      "height": 311.0,
      "z_value": -9.883000000000065,
      "ports_visible": false,
      "shelf_visible": true,
      "body_text": "E-commerce and Product\n46. Product description generator. A spec sheet in. On brand descriptions for the full catalog out, formatted for your platform.\n47. Review response drafter. Drafts replies to new reviews in your brand voice, flagging anything that needs a human first.\n48. Inventory reorder alert. Compares stock to sales velocity, drafts reorder recommendations before anything runs out.\n49. Pricing monitor. Tracks competitor pricing and summarizes the moment something shifts.\n50. Abandoned cart sequence. Drafts a tailored recovery message for each cart based on what was in it and how far the buyer got."
    },
    {
      "node_id": 0,
      "node_type": "warm",
      "title": "Soft",
      "uuid": "17f5751ecc1f4f41b1c86d422bc6215d",
      "x": 4400.336174747763,
      "y": 1787.1614350574387,
      "width": 300.0,
      "height": 266.0,
      "z_value": -9.882000000000065,
      "ports_visible": false,
      "shelf_visible": true,
      "body_text": "Finance and Admin\n51. Invoice drafter. When a project closes, it drafts the invoice from the scope you set at the start.\n52. Expense categorizer. A month of transactions in. Categorized, with anything unusual flagged, out.\n53. Contract first read. Reads an incoming contract and flags unusual clauses and missing terms before your lawyer bills you.\n54. Subscription auditor. A scheduled review that flags subscriptions you are not using.\n55. Cash flow briefing. A weekly summary of what came in, what went out, and what is due."
    },
    {
      "node_id": 0,
      "node_type": "warm",
      "title": "Bright Tomorrow",
      "uuid": "f880d21575504381aa428547a0c3d7fb",
      "x": 4345.529598643713,
      "y": 2090.8629600819318,
      "width": 300.0,
      "height": 326.0,
      "z_value": -9.881000000000066,
      "ports_visible": false,
      "shelf_visible": true,
      "body_text": "Niche and Specialized\n56. Job posting to outreach. Scans job boards for companies your product solves a problem for, drafts outreach explaining why they need you now.\n57. Grant and application scanner. Scans funding opportunities pre filtered against your eligibility, drafts an application for strong matches.\n58. Podcast guest research packet. Compiles a packet on the guest, their recent work, and three questions nobody else has asked them.\n59. Brand consistency checker. A weekly scan flagging contradictions, repeated claims, or drift from your positioning.\n60. Localization pipeline. Adapts your best content for a different market, localized for tone and reference, not just translated."
    },
    {
      "node_id": 0,
      "node_type": "warm",
      "title": "Onward",
      "uuid": "8ba707ea64084be29e0b1b69bf6d0844",
      "x": 4421.380365057757,
      "y": 580.9895823696932,
      "width": 300.0,
      "height": 341.0,
      "z_value": -9.887000000000063,
      "ports_visible": false,
      "shelf_visible": true,
      "body_text": "Multi Agent Systems\n26. Maker and checker pair. One agent writes. A second reviews it against a checklist before it reaches you.\n27. Debate panel. Run the same question through multiple models and have them critique each other before a final synthesis.\n28. Research and writer split. One agent gathers and verifies facts. A second, with no search access, writes using only what was confirmed. Fabricated claims never reach the draft.\n29. Triage and escalation. A cheap fast model handles routine classification. Fable 5 handles only what actually needs the depth.\n30. Self auditing loop. After finishing, the agent reviews its own output against the original instructions before marking it done."
    },
    {
      "node_id": 0,
      "node_type": "warm",
      "title": "Soft",
      "uuid": "537fbc5895c34ce7977bf2336e08300f",
      "x": 4004.8349260398068,
      "y": 805.3969624219908,
      "width": 300.0,
      "height": 296.0,
      "z_value": -9.886000000000063,
      "ports_visible": false,
      "shelf_visible": true,
      "body_text": "Customer Support and Community\n31. Support first responder. Drafts a reply to every ticket using your knowledge base. Nobody starts from a blank box.\n32. Community onboarding flow. New members get an interactive conversation at any hour, with no one on your team awake.\n33. FAQ gap finder. A weekly review that surfaces questions your FAQ does not answer.\n34. Refund and dispute drafter. For cases meeting your policy, it drafts the resolution and internal note, ready to approve in seconds.\n35. Sentiment monitor. A daily scan that flags any spike in negative sentiment before it becomes visible."
    },
    {
      "node_id": 0,
      "node_type": "warm",
      "title": "Gentle",
      "uuid": "bda1f17bee9d43e1be245471d947617b",
      "x": 4049.9376784504957,
      "y": 1203.381315561346,
      "width": 300.0,
      "height": 311.0,
      "z_value": -9.885000000000064,
      "ports_visible": false,
      "shelf_visible": true,
      "body_text": "Personal Productivity Systems\n36. Daily briefing generator. Before you wake, it compiles your calendar, important unread email, and relevant news into one briefing.\n37. Email triage assistant. Reads your inbox overnight, drafts replies to quick items, sorts the rest by urgency.\n38. Weekly review compiler. Every Sunday it pulls from your notes, calendar, and completed tasks to draft a review.\n39. Reading list processor. Everything you saved this week, summarized into one digest.\n40. Goal tracker review. A weekly check against your metrics, flagging trends and asking the one question that matters from the data."
    },
    {
      "node_id": 0,
      "node_type": "warm",
      "title": "Grace",
      "uuid": "b0de6cc780e9415c81b320d12fcd4f50",
      "x": 4580.284872786988,
      "y": 3031.908556130141,
      "width": 300.0,
      "height": 371.0,
      "z_value": -10.0,
      "ports_visible": false,
      "shelf_visible": true,
      "body_text": "How to Actually Start\nPick one workflow. The one that maps onto something you already do every single day.\nGet it running reliably for a week before adding a second. Reliable means it ran without you checking constantly and the output was good enough to use without heavy editing. Still rewriting most of it? Not done. Just looks done.\nThe people who build five fragile systems babysit all five. The people who build one solid system build the next nine faster, because they already know exactly what breaks and why.\nThat compounding is the real unlock. Not the first workflow. The tenth, built in a fraction of the time the first one took.\nPick one. Build it properly. The other fifty nine get easier once the first one actually runs.\nFollow @cyrilXBT for the full builds behind each of these."
    },
    {
      "node_id": 0,
      "node_type": "warm",
      "title": "Good",
      "uuid": "4aff080ffc464f3197ea1ac900d40cfa",
      "x": 3975.7097480487782,
      "y": 2307.2438939697163,
      "width": 300.0,
      "height": 356.0,
      "z_value": -9.880000000000067,
      "ports_visible": false,
      "shelf_visible": true,
      "body_text": "Why Fable 5 Specifically\nYou could run some of these on Opus 4.8. So why does Fable 5 change the game.\nBecause the workflows that actually make money while you sleep are the long ones. The overnight repo sweep that touches forty files. The research agent that spawns six sub agents and synthesizes them. The multi stage pipeline that runs for three hours without a human checkpoint.\nOpus 4.8 fragments on tasks that long. Fable 5 was trained to hold intent and context across them. It plans before acting. It checks its own work. It expands context as the task demands instead of losing the thread halfway through.\nThat is the entire reason a task you used to break into five sessions now runs as one unattended job. And a task that runs as one unattended job is a task you can leave running while you sleep."
    },
    {
      "node_id": 0,
      "node_type": "warm",
      "title": "Delicate",
      "uuid": "fc8da3c2f45f41579b333417c6210d98",
      "x": 4451.743287019951,
      "y": 2609.074716501813,
      "width": 300.0,
      "height": 356.0,
      "z_value": -9.879000000000067,
      "ports_visible": false,
      "shelf_visible": true,
      "body_text": "The Pattern Behind All Sixty\nNone of these are Fable 5 outsmarting you. They are Fable 5 removing you from work that never needed your judgment in real time.\nEvery one runs on the same three parts.\nA trigger. A schedule, a new file, an incoming message.\nA capable agent. Fable 5 with the right tools, the right context, and the audit instruction from the top of this article.\nA verification step. Something checking the output before it reaches you. A test suite, a second agent, a rule based filter.\nMiss the verification and you get expensive mistakes running all night. Miss the trigger and you are starting everything by hand again. Build all three and you have a system that genuinely works overnight, not a demo that works while you watch."
    },
    {
      "node_id": 0,
      "node_type": "warm",
      "title": "Bright Tomorrow",
      "uuid": "b2c53e48416b4d9a8bda2988b1106e37",
      "x": 110.74436481757493,
      "y": -554.62563307852,
      "width": 369.8959786043121,
      "height": 1615.9686561915905,
      "z_value": -9.906000000000052,
      "ports_visible": false,
      "shelf_visible": true,
      "body_text": "A man on a donkey in 1635\nThe scent of a flower on a Thursday\nThe sound a candle makes\nA spoon dropped in a cathedral\nFirst breath after swimming underwater\nThe third exhale of a long story\nA child's forgotten birthday wish\nMoonrise viewed from a kitchen window\nThe taste of tap water at 3am\nA door closing behind someone you love\nThe first hour of a snowstorm\nA grandmother's lullaby half-remembered\nCoffee steam on a bathroom mirror\nThe weight of an unopened letter\nFootsteps in an empty hallway\nA postman pausing before knocking\nThe smell of rain on hot pavement\nA letter that arrived eleven days late\nThe last verse of a song nobody sings anymore\nA dog dreaming\nSunday afternoon in a library with no one in it\nA forgotten pocket in a winter coat\nThe quiet between two fireworks\nTea steeping for far too long\nAn old map folded wrong\nThe first cricket of summer\nA stranger's laugh overheard on a train\nA child counting stars out loud\nThe click of a film camera in the next room\nA train whistle at 4am\nThe last page of a book you didn't want to end\nA kettle about to boil\nRain hitting a window at night\nA cat watching traffic from a fourth-floor sill\nThe breath between two lines of poetry\nA balloon caught in a winter tree\nA telephone ringing in a film\nA candle three seconds after blowing out\nThe pause before a joke lands\nA passport lost and then found\nSnow melting on a hat you forgot you were wearing\nRust on a bicycle chain in April\nThe moment before applause\nA pigeon landing on a statue's head\nThe smell of a library book from 1974\nA broken promise, nonetheless kept\nA forgotten umbrella at a wedding\nThe last warm day of September\nA hand waving goodbye through a train window\nA bird flying into a room and out again\nThe taste of your own name said by a stranger\nA childhood pet's name\nThe sound a memory makes when it almost surfaces\nCoffee gone cold on a desk\nA cake baking in an empty house\nAn email sent to exactly the wrong person\nFootprints in wet sand being erased\nA mother calling you home for dinner\nThe shadow of a moth on a lampshade\nA coin spinning on a wooden table\nA subway arriving empty at midnight\nThe warmth of a laptop on your legs\nA doorway that only exists in a dream\nThe echo of a church bell at dusk\nA neighbor practicing piano badly\nAn aunt's kitchen in the summer of 1992\nWind pushing a screen door gently\nA recipe written in pencil on an index card\nA photograph deleted accidentally\nThe weight of words you didn't say\nA puddle reflecting a cloud\nA conversation with your eighteen-year-old self\nThe last letter of a foreign alphabet\nA friend who moved away and stayed away\nDust dancing in a beam of sunlight\nThe kind of silence only cats know\nA freshly-made hotel bed\nThe gap between a breath and a sigh\nThe hum of a refrigerator at night\nA wedding ring left on a bedside table\nA library card from 1987\nA child's drawing held up by a magnet\nA chair that remembers the shape of someone\nThe breeze from a ceiling fan in August\nA grandfather clock ticking in a dark hallway\nThe inside of a snowglobe mid-shake\nAn unpaid utility bill\nThe first frost on grass\nA song stuck in someone else's head\nA book you keep meaning to read\nThe smell of sunscreen on a cold day\nA bowl of cherries in midsummer\nA taxi passing through rain without stopping\nThe sound of a zipper in a quiet room\nA cardboard box with no return address\nAn umbrella on a coat rack in June\nThe taste of homesickness\nA paper crane on a windowsill\nA melody heard only in dreams\nThe moment you realize a page is ending"
    }
  ],
